## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of bit pattern encoding, branchless 64-bit manipulation, and mask-driven bit dilation techniques. It evaluates consistency across endianness and fixed-width arithmetic skills.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless bit manipulation
- Fixed-width masks for progressive dilation
- 64-bit hexadecimal mask constants
- Bit-level reasoning and positional mapping

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t spread32to64(uint32_t x) {
    uint64_t y = x;

    // Progressive dilation using fixed masks
    y = (y | (y << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
    y = (y | (y << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
    y = (y | (y << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
    y = (y | (y << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
    y = (y | (y << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

    // Duplicate each bit to adjacent position
    return y | (y << 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

